W={
'b2w1':[(L2,'Social media has changed the way young people communicate, learn and spend their free time. In my view, it has done more harm than good.\n\nIt is true that social media makes it easier to stay in touch with friends. However, many teenagers now spend hours scrolling through messages instead of meeting face to face, and these online conversations are often shallow.\n\nThe effect on mental health is even more worrying. Young people are constantly exposed to edited photos and seemingly perfect lives, which can make them feel that they are not good enough. Studies have linked heavy use to anxiety, poor sleep and low self-esteem.\n\nA further problem is that social media is designed to be addictive. Endless feeds and notifications make it extremely difficult to concentrate on homework or reading, and many students admit that they check their phones dozens of times an hour.\n\nIn conclusion, although social media has some benefits, I believe its negative effects on young people\'s well-being and concentration are far more significant. Parents and schools need to do much more to help teenagers use it in moderation.'),
 (L3,'Few inventions have transformed teenage life as dramatically as social media, and its impact remains fiercely debated.\n\nAdmittedly, it has made staying connected almost effortless. Friendships no longer end when someone moves to another city, and young people who feel isolated – perhaps because of their interests or identity – can find communities where they are accepted.\n\nNevertheless, the cost to mental health cannot be ignored. Platforms reward appearance and popularity, encouraging constant comparison. Many teenagers measure their worth in likes, and when the likes do not come, the consequences can be painful.\n\nWhat is perhaps less often discussed is the impact on attention. Short videos and endless feeds train the brain to expect constant stimulation, making it harder to focus on longer tasks such as reading a novel or revising for an exam.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that social media itself is neither good nor bad; the harm comes from unlimited, uncritical use. If young people were taught from an early age how these platforms work and how to set limits, they could enjoy the advantages while avoiding the worst of the risks.')],
'b2w2':[(L2,'Every summer, millions of tourists visit popular destinations, and local people are increasingly asking whether this is really good for them.\n\nOf course, tourism brings money and jobs. Hotels, restaurants and tour companies employ thousands of people, and many small businesses depend entirely on visitors. Without tourism, some coastal towns would have very little income.\n\nHowever, this money comes at a price. Popular beaches are often covered in rubbish, and new hotels are built on land that was once home to wildlife. In some places, there is not even enough water for residents during the busy season.\n\nAnother serious problem is the effect on everyday life. As more flats are rented to tourists, rents rise and young local people can no longer afford to live in their own neighbourhoods. Traditional shops are replaced by souvenir stalls.\n\nIn my opinion, tourism in its current form does more harm than good to many places. Governments should limit the number of visitors to the most crowded destinations and make sure that tourist money is used to protect the environment and support local communities.'),
 (L3,'Tourism is often presented as a straightforward success story: visitors arrive, spend money and leave happy. The reality, however, is considerably more complicated.\n\nThere is no denying the economic benefits. Tourism generates employment, supports local crafts and can fund the restoration of historic buildings that might otherwise fall into ruin. For developing regions in particular, it can be a lifeline.\n\nYet the environmental consequences are often severe. Fragile ecosystems such as coral reefs and mountain trails are damaged by overuse, while the flights that bring tourists contribute significantly to climate change.\n\nPerhaps the most overlooked issue is cultural. When a town reshapes itself entirely around visitors\' expectations, it risks losing the very authenticity that attracted them in the first place. Festivals become performances, and residents begin to feel like extras in their own home.\n\nUltimately, I believe tourism can benefit the places people visit, but only if it is managed with the long term in mind. Limiting numbers, encouraging travel outside peak season and ensuring that profits stay in the local community would go a long way towards achieving this balance.')],
'b2w3':[(L2,'A weekend in the city that never sleeps\n\nWhile some people dream of peaceful beaches, my perfect weekend would be the opposite: two days in a big, busy city like Istanbul with my three closest friends.\n\nWe would arrive on Friday evening and go straight to a rooftop restaurant with a view of the Bosphorus. After dinner, we would wander through the narrow streets, stopping for tea and listening to street musicians.\n\nOn Saturday, we would get up early – well, not too early! – and visit the Grand Bazaar, where we could practise our bargaining skills. In the afternoon, we would take a ferry to the Asian side, eat fish sandwiches by the water and watch the sunset.\n\nSunday would be for museums and, of course, more food. I would make sure we tried at least five different desserts.\n\nIt might sound exhausting, but for me there is nothing more exciting than exploring a new place with people who make me laugh. By Sunday night, I would be tired but completely happy.'),
 (L3,'The art of doing absolutely nothing\n\nWe are constantly told to make the most of our free time: learn a language, run a marathon, see the world. My idea of a perfect weekend, however, is gloriously unproductive.\n\nI would spend it at my grandparents\' house in a small village in the Black Sea mountains, where the phone signal is unreliable and nobody seems to be in a hurry. My only company would be my grandparents, their elderly cat and perhaps my younger brother, provided he left his games console at home.\n\nI would wake up without an alarm to the smell of fresh bread and spend the morning reading in the garden, surrounded by tea plants and mist. In the afternoon, I might help my grandfather in his vegetable garden, although I suspect I would mostly be listening to his stories about the village fifty years ago.\n\nEvenings would be spent around the stove, playing cards and eating far too much.\n\nIt may not sound exciting, but in a world that never stops, I think the greatest luxury of all is having time to slow down.')],
'b2w4':[(L2,'Breaking news, broken hearts: Newsroom\n\nNewsroom, a drama about a group of young journalists working for a struggling newspaper, is one of the most gripping series I have watched this year.\n\nEach episode follows a different news story, from a political scandal to a missing child, but the real focus is on the characters and the difficult choices they face. Should they publish a story that is true but could ruin someone\'s life? How far should they go to get the truth?\n\nThe writing is sharp and often surprisingly funny, and the cast is excellent. I particularly enjoyed the relationship between the experienced editor and the ambitious new reporter, which is both touching and realistic.\n\nOn the negative side, the final season feels rushed, and some storylines are resolved too quickly. A few of the newsroom scenes are also rather unrealistic – I doubt real journalists have time for so many dramatic arguments!\n\nDespite these weaknesses, I would strongly recommend Newsroom to anyone who enjoys intelligent dramas with strong characters. It will also make you think twice about the news you read every day.'),
 (L3,'Small town, big secrets: a review of Hollow Creek\n\nAt first glance, Hollow Creek appears to be yet another crime series set in a sleepy village. Within the first ten minutes, however, it becomes clear that this is something rather more ambitious.\n\nThe story begins when a respected local teacher disappears on the night of the annual summer festival. Rather than following the investigation from the police\'s point of view, each episode is told through the eyes of a different villager, gradually revealing how their lives – and lies – are connected.\n\nWhat makes the series stand out is its patience. The director is not afraid of long, silent scenes, and the stunning landscape becomes almost a character in itself. The performances are uniformly strong, although the young actor playing the teacher\'s son deserves particular praise for a remarkably subtle performance.\n\nThat said, the slow pace will not suit everyone, and the final twist, while clever, stretches belief somewhat.\n\nAll in all, Hollow Creek is a thoughtful, beautifully made series that rewards viewers who are willing to pay attention. Fans of fast-moving action may be disappointed, but anyone who enjoys complex characters will be hooked.')],
'b2w5':[(L2,'Hi Chris,\nThanks for your email. It\'s great that you\'re thinking about getting a summer job – I learned so much from mine.\n\nLast summer I worked at a children\'s summer camp near the lake. I helped organise games, took the kids swimming and made sure everyone was safe. It was exhausting, especially when it rained and thirty children were stuck indoors!\n\nThe most important thing I learned was patience. Some children were homesick or didn\'t want to join in, and I had to find ways to help them feel comfortable. I also became much more confident about speaking in front of groups.\n\nIf I were you, I\'d think about what you enjoy before choosing. You love being outdoors, so maybe a job at a campsite or a sports centre would suit you. Also, try to get some experience that will look good on your CV – employers really value it.\n\nOne last piece of advice: don\'t forget to have some fun too. Make sure you have at least a couple of weeks free at the end of summer.\n\nGood luck!\nSelin'),
 (L3,'Hi Chris,\nWhat a great idea! I\'m sure a summer job will be a brilliant experience, even if it\'s hard work at times.\n\nAs you know, last summer I worked as an assistant in a small bookshop in the old town. I mainly served customers and arranged displays, but I also helped organise a couple of events with local authors, which was the highlight for me.\n\nThe job taught me far more than I expected. I discovered that I\'m better at dealing with people than I thought, even the occasional rude customer! I also learned how a small business actually works – how much planning goes into ordering stock, for example – which has made me consider studying business at university.\n\nAs for advice, my main suggestion would be to choose something that might help you in the future, rather than simply the job that pays the most. Since you\'re interested in design, why not ask local studios or print shops whether they need help? Even if they don\'t advertise, they might be happy to take on someone enthusiastic.\n\nWhatever you choose, I\'m sure you\'ll do brilliantly. Let me know how it goes!\nBest wishes,\nDeniz')],
}
S={
'b2s1':[
 ["On weekdays, I tend to spend most evenings at home studying, although I always try to take a break to have dinner with my family. If I've finished my homework early, I might go for a walk or meet a friend for a coffee. On Fridays, we usually watch a film together, which is a nice way to start the weekend.",
  "My evenings are quite busy, actually. Three times a week I go to football training, which finishes at about eight. After that, I'm usually too tired to do much, so I just eat, chat with my friends online and maybe watch an episode of a series before bed."],
 ["I'd say I prefer reading, mainly because I can use my imagination. When I read, I picture the characters and places in my own way, whereas films sometimes disappoint me if they're different from what I expected. Having said that, films are better when I'm tired and just want to relax.",
  "To be honest, I prefer films. I spend a lot of time reading for school, so in my free time I want something more relaxing. I also love the music and the visual effects in films – a good soundtrack can make a story so much more powerful."],
 ["I've always wanted to learn to sail. My uncle has a small boat on the coast, and every summer he promises to teach me, but we never seem to find the time. Being out on the open sea, completely in control of the boat, must be an amazing feeling.",
  "I'd really like to learn Japanese. I'm a big fan of Japanese films and food, and I'm hoping to visit Japan after university. I know it's a difficult language, especially the writing, but I think the challenge would make it even more rewarding."],
 ["Quite important, actually. I find that if I don't have a routine, I waste a lot of time deciding what to do and end up stressed before exams. Having fixed times for studying, exercise and sleep makes me more productive, and paradoxically gives me more free time.",
  "Not very, to be honest. I get bored if every day is the same, so I prefer to plan my week loosely and decide things as I go. Of course, I need some routine for school, but at the weekends I like to be spontaneous."]],
'b2s2':[
 ["Both photos show people learning, but in quite different settings. In the first one, there's a traditional classroom, with a teacher explaining something on the board and students taking notes, whereas in the second, a young man seems to be following an online lesson at home, wearing headphones. One advantage of the classroom is that students can ask questions immediately and learn from each other through discussion. The teacher can also see when someone is confused. In contrast, learning online is much more flexible. The young man can probably study whenever he wants and watch the lesson again if he doesn't understand something. I suppose he also saves time and money on travelling. However, he might find it harder to stay motivated without other people around him.",
  "These two photographs both show ways of learning, but the atmosphere is very different. The classroom looks lively and quite formal – the students are sitting in rows and paying attention to the teacher. By contrast, the young man at home looks relaxed, sitting at his desk with a cup of coffee. I think the main advantage of learning in a classroom is the social side: you make friends, you can work in groups and you feel part of a community. Learning online, on the other hand, allows you to work at your own speed and choose exactly what you want to study. It's probably ideal for people who work or live far from a school."],
 ["I'd go for the classroom, mainly because I find it hard to concentrate on my own – I get distracted by my phone far too easily. I also enjoy discussing ideas with my classmates, which helps me remember things better.",
  "Personally, I prefer learning online. I'm quite an independent person, and I like being able to pause the lesson, take notes and go back over the difficult parts as many times as I need. It also means I can fit my studies around other things, like sport or a part-time job."]],
'b2s3':[
 ["Both photographs show people celebrating, but the scale and atmosphere are completely different. The first one shows a family birthday party at home, with a big cake, balloons and lots of relatives around a table, while the second shows a huge crowd watching fireworks in a city square, probably on New Year's Eve. The family might be celebrating at home because it feels more personal – they can talk, share food and spend quality time together. It also seems quite relaxed and informal. The people in the city, on the other hand, perhaps want to feel part of something bigger. There's something exciting about counting down with thousands of strangers, and the fireworks and music create an atmosphere you can't get at home.",
  "In the first picture, a family is celebrating a birthday in a living room, whereas the second picture shows a big public celebration with fireworks over a city. I imagine the family chose to celebrate at home because they want to make the person whose birthday it is feel special, and perhaps because there are young children and elderly relatives who might not enjoy a crowd. The people in the city are probably celebrating a national holiday or New Year, and they've come out to enjoy the spectacle together. Big events like that make people feel connected to their community, even if they don't know anyone around them."],
 ["I'd rather celebrate at home with my family. Big crowds make me a bit nervous, and I prefer having real conversations with people I care about. Also, the food is always better at home! And honestly, I think the best memories come from small, personal moments rather than big public events.",
  "I'd probably choose the city celebration. I love the energy of big events, and watching fireworks with thousands of people is an experience you remember for years. Home celebrations are lovely, but they can feel quite similar from year to year."]],
'b2s4':[
 ["Let's start with the history museum. Students could learn about the past in a much more memorable way than from a textbook, especially if there are interactive exhibitions. A factory visit might sound boring, but it would show students how everyday things are actually made and what kinds of jobs exist, which could help them think about their future careers. The national park would be great for learning about nature and the environment, and students would get some fresh air and exercise too. A theatre trip could help students understand literature better – seeing a play performed is very different from reading it in class. Finally, a science centre is usually very hands-on, so students learn by doing experiments themselves, which is probably the most effective way to learn. I think each place has something to offer, but it depends on the age of the students and what they're studying at the moment.",
  "I'll begin with the theatre. It's a great way to make literature come alive, and many students have never been to a real theatre, so it would be a new experience for them. The science centre is fun and educational, though some can be a bit childish for older students. The history museum is a classic choice; it's informative, but students might get bored if they just look at objects behind glass. A factory is interesting because it connects school subjects like chemistry or engineering to the real world. And the national park would teach students about ecosystems and environmental protection, and it would also be good for team building, since students would have to work together on activities like orienteering. So there are clear benefits to all of them. The question is really which one would offer the most to the greatest number of students, and whether the cost and travel time would be reasonable. A trip that's too far away might mean students spend more time on the bus than actually learning."],
 ["I think the best option would be the science centre, because it's both educational and entertaining, and students of all ages would enjoy it. Students learn best when they're actively involved, and a science centre allows them to experiment and ask questions. The national park would be my second choice, although it depends a lot on the weather. Another advantage of the science centre is that it works well for mixed groups: students who aren't very academic often enjoy practical activities more than lessons, so everyone would get something out of it.",
  "For me, the national park is the clear winner. Students spend most of their time indoors looking at screens, so a day outside would be really valuable. They'd learn about nature, get some exercise and probably talk to classmates they don't normally spend time with. It would also be relatively cheap, since entrance to most national parks is free, and teachers could organise activities linked to biology and geography lessons so that the day still has a clear educational purpose."]],
'b2s5':[
 ["In some ways, yes. For instance, you learn teamwork from playing sport, responsibility from part-time jobs and confidence from travelling or meeting new people. These are things that are difficult to teach in a classroom. However, I'd argue that the classroom is still essential for building basic knowledge, like maths, science and languages. Ideally, schools should combine both.",
  "I don't think so. Outside the classroom, learning is often random – you might learn useful things, but you might also learn nothing at all. In a classroom, teachers plan lessons carefully so that knowledge builds step by step. Without that structure, most students would have big gaps in their education. Of course, experiences outside school are valuable, but they work best when they build on what students have already learned in class."],
 ["I'm not convinced. On the one hand, students would be less tired and have more time for hobbies and sport, which is good for their mental health. On the other hand, many of them would probably just spend the extra time on their phones. Also, if the school day were shorter, teachers would have to cover the same material in less time, which could increase stress.",
  "Yes, I think they should. Many students are at school until late afternoon and then have hours of homework, so they have almost no free time. Shorter days would give them time to rest, exercise and pursue interests like music or art, which are just as important for their development. Some countries with shorter school days, like Finland, still achieve excellent results, so it's clearly possible."],
 ["I imagine technology is likely to play a much bigger role. Lessons will probably be more personalised, with programs adapting to each student's level and speed. Students might also learn more through virtual reality, for example visiting ancient Rome in a history lesson. But I think teachers will still be needed to motivate students and help them develop social skills.",
  "I think there'll be a bigger focus on practical skills, like problem solving, communication and managing money, rather than memorising facts, since everyone can look up information instantly. Exams might also change, with more projects and less pressure on a single test at the end of the year. I think that would be fairer, because some students perform badly under exam pressure even when they understand the subject well."]]}
